'''''Tulbaghia violacea''''', commonly known as '''society garlic''', '''pink agapanthus''',{{r|floridata793}} '''wild garlic''', '''sweet garlic''', '''spring bulbs''', or '''spring flowers''',{{r|gardeniaT_violacea}} is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the family ''[[Amaryllidaceae]]''.<ref name=POWO>{{cite web |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:542801-1#synonyms |title=Tulbaghia violacea Harv. |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=n.d. |website=Plants of the World Online |publisher=The Trustees of the Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew |access-date=24 November 2024}}</ref><ref name=COL>{{cite web |url=https://www.catalogueoflife.org/data/taxon/59KTR |title=Tulbaghia violacea Harv. |author=<!--Not stated--> |date=n.d. |website=Catalogue of Life |publisher=Species 2000 |access-date=24 November 2024}}</ref> It is [[native plant|indigenous]] to southern Africa ([[KwaZulu-Natal]] and [[Cape Province]]), and reportedly naturalized in Tanzania and Mexico.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:542801-1 |title=''Tulbaghia violacea'' Harv. |website=Kew World Checklist of Selected Plant Families}}</ref>

Growing to {{convert|60|cm|0|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|25|cm|0|abbr=on}} wide, it is a clump-forming [[perennial plant|perennial]] with narrow leaves and large clusters of fragrant, violet flowers from midsummer to autumn (fall).<ref name=RHSAZ>{{cite book |title=RHS A-Z encyclopedia of garden plants |year=2008 |publisher=Dorling Kindersley |location=United Kingdom |isbn=978-1405332965 |pages=1136}}</ref><ref>Harvey, William Henry 1837. Botanical Magazine 64: t. 3555.</ref>

==Description and cultivation==
When grown as an ornamental, this plant requires some protection from winter frosts. This species<ref>{{cite web |title=RHS Plant Selector - ''Tulbaghia violacea'' |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/18484/Tulbaghia-violacea/Details |publisher=RHS |access-date=5 March 2021}}</ref> and the [[cultivars]] 'Purple Eye'<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/308723/i-Tulbaghia-i-Purple-Eye/Details |title=RHS Plantfinder - ''Tulbaghia'' 'Purple Eye' |access-date=29 December 2018}}</ref> and 'Silver Lace', with cream-margined leaves,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/74071/i-Tulbaghia-violacea-i-Silver-Lace-(v)/Details |title=RHS Plantfinder - ''Tulbaghia violacea'' 'Silver Lace' |access-date=27 December 2018}}</ref> have all gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf |title=AGM Plants - Ornamental |date=July 2017 |page=103 |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society |access-date=27 December 2018}}</ref>

Treatment of seeds with a smoke solution has been shown to increase the leaf mass and height of ''T. violacea'' seedlings. Seeds exposed to aerosol smoke was also shown to lead to a higher seedling survival percentage.{{r|sparg2005improvingseedlingvigour}}

===Odour===
While the smell of ''T. violacea'' is typically described as garlic like,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://plants.ces.ncsu.edu/plants/tulbaghia-violacea/ |website=North Carolina Extension Gardener Plant Toolbox |publisher=North Carolina State Extension |title=''Tulbaghia violacea'' |access-date=1 June 2023}}</ref> there has been an instance where police were called about the smell of [[marijuana|cannabis]] in a neighborhood only to find out that the culprit was actually a combination of [[lemon verbena]] and society garlic.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/Mystery-Smell-In-Weston-94033484.html |title=Mystery Smell in Weston |last=Kuperstein |first=Adam |date=18 May 2010 |website=NBC Miami |archive-url= |archive-date= |access-date=}}</ref>

==Uses==
===Culinary uses===
''T. violacea'' leaves are eaten as a substitute for chives and garlic. In South Africa, [[Zulu people]] eat the leaves and flowers as a leaf vegetable like spinach or for seasoning meat and potatoes.{{r|aremu2013thegenusTulbaghia}}

===Medicinal uses===
''Tulbaghia violacea'' is used locally as a herbal remedy/medicine to treat several ailments.{{r|aremu2013thegenusTulbaghia}}

Due to increasing evidence of its potential as an antifungal agent, large-scale commercialization is anticipated. However, this may make the ''[[Tulbaghia]]'' genus threatened as it is susceptible to overuse.{{r|aremu2013thegenusTulbaghia}}

===Safety and toxicity===
Some fatalities and symptoms like gastro-enteritis, abdominal pain, cessation of gastro-intestinal peristalsis, sloughing of the intestinal mucosa, and contraction of the pupils, have been implicated in medication prepared with ''T. violacea''. There has been speculation that ''T. violacea'' may cause poisonings but tests on rabbit showed no negative effects. It is possible that reported adverse effects are due to extensive use and/or high dosages of the plant. Adverse effects are generally assumed to be associated with the steroidal saponins and/or the sulphur compounds.{{r|aremu2013thegenusTulbaghia}}

Ncube et al. (2011) found that the leaves and flowers of the plant are edible as vegetables. Elgorashi et al. (2003) used the Ames and VITOTOX tests and found that these parts (leaves and flowers) are non-toxic.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Madike |first1=Lerato Nellvecia |last2=Takaidza |first2=Samkeliso |last3=Ssemakalu |first3=Cornelius C. |last4=Pillay |first4=Michael |date=1 May 2020 |title=The effect of extracts of Tulbaghia violacea on the proliferation of a murine macrophage cell line |journal=South African Journal of Botany |language=en |volume=130 |pages=185–197 |doi=10.1016/j.sajb.2019.12.025 |s2cid=213232640 |issn=0254-6299 |doi-access=free}}</ref>

